Mild perianal itching and discomfort secondary to aggressive mechanical wiping
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Perianal intense pruritus, Linear radiating excoriations, Wiping-exacerbated burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe perianal pain and burning mimicking thrombosed hemorrhoids or perianal abscess.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted intense, escalating perianal pain and pruritus. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated prior to the next in-hours period to rule out a thrombosed external hemorrhoid or a developing perianal abscess, which require urgent surgical drainage or specialized medical management to prevent severe morbidity.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My backside is burning, itching intensely, and it hurts terribly when I go to the bathroom.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports intense pruritus localized purely to the perianal skin, developing over the past 48 hours. Describes a transient burning sensation heavily exacerbated by defecation or further wiping with harsh textured toilet paper. Notes localized mild perianal erythema and superficial linear excoriations radiating from the anal verge. Complete absence of active gross rectal bleeding, fever, or palpable perianal masses.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, sitting is uncomfortable.
Social/Safety Concerns: Reluctance to defecate due to anticipated pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/82 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, shifting weight frequently while seated.
Physical Exam: Perianal examination reveals localized mild perianal erythema with superficial linear excoriations radiating from the anal verge. Diagnostic triad confirmed: intense perianal pruritus, linear radiating excoriations, and history of wiping-exacerbated burning. Absence of palpable internal or external hemorrhoidal disease. Intact baseline anal sphincter tone. Normal rectal vault on digital examination without fluctuance, masses, or tenderness. Negative for pinworms on gross inspection.
Environmental Observations: Rough, non-ply toilet tissue noted in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Overuse of over-the-counter medicated hemorrhoid wipes containing alcohol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild perianal itching and discomfort secondary to aggressive mechanical wiping (Pruritus Ani) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Thrombosed External Hemorrhoid - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of a tense, tender, purplish palpable nodule at the anal verge.

Perianal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized fluctuance, deep induration, and systemic fever; normal digital rectal examination.

Pinworm Infection (Enterobiasis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pruritus directly correlated to mechanical wiping rather than primarily nocturnal itching, and absence of visible nematodes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute perianal burning and pain necessitates a direct physical evaluation to exclude serious anorectal pathology. Delaying the assessment until the next in-hours period risked missing a perianal abscess or thrombosed hemorrhoid, both of which require prompt intervention to prevent sepsis or severe ischemic pain. The urgent physical and digital rectal examination confidently confirmed superficial mechanical excoriation, allowing for immediate de-escalation of care and safe, targeted symptomatic management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to cease use of all medicated wipes and harsh toilet paper. Prescription for a short course of topical 1% hydrocortisone ointment mixed with zinc oxide barrier cream applied sparingly twice daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use an unscented baby wipe or a bidet attachment for post-defecation cleansing, followed by gently patting the area dry with a soft towel, to break the cycle of mechanical friction and epidermal stripping.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks if symptoms have not completely resolved.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a high fever, extreme unremitting rectal pain, inability to pass urine, or large amounts of blood in your stool.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is superficial mechanical irritation; persistent symptoms may require further gastroenterological workup.
Clinical Course: Burning and itching should dramatically improve within 3 to 5 days of eliminating the mechanical friction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you feel a hard, intensely painful lump develop near the anus, or if you begin passing significant amounts of bright red blood.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Vigorous wiping with rough paper has stripped the protective top layer of skin, leaving raw nerve endings exposed to acidic stool. The barrier cream protects the raw skin, and the mild steroid reduces the itch.
Actionable Steps: Never rub the area dry; always pat gently. Wash with plain water rather than using scented soaps or alcohol-based wipes.

SMS INFORMATION

